    "body": "I'm not following the logic here. The purpose of nosniff is to treat the Content-Type header as authoritative. If Content-Type is not present, or otherwise has an unuseful value, nosniff becomes moot and is used only to prevent a privilege escalation. The purpose of the \"scriptable\" annotation remains relevant for security.\r\n\r\nI don't know where `images/blue.png` is now, but sniffing images is not considered security-sensitive. If your claim on browser behavior holds, it sounds to me like Firefox is following the spec just fine. And Safari's behavior _could_ be safe if it decided not to sniff for images (or declare support for images) in this context. I have no idea why Chrome would detect a PNG image as \"text/plain\" in any context, given that even the signature contains binary bytes.",
